<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Garamantian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Garamantes, Garamantian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Garamantian Empire was a state of the Garamantes, a Berber-descended people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, which flourished from around the first millennium BCE to the early centuries CE. It built a network of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foggara">foggara</a> tunnels to tap fossil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> and controlled trans-Saharan trade, and it declined as water levels fell and trade collapsed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire.jpg" alt="Garamantian Empire" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrein · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_125.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Garamantes may have descended from Libyco-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes, Toubou tribes and Saharan pastoralists who settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> at least by 1000 BCE. At the Takarkori rockshelter, Final Pastoral peoples created burial sites for several hundred individuals around 3000 BP, with luxury goods from elsewhere, which made way for the Garamantian kingdom. From the 6th century BCE the Garamantes formed a powerful polity in northern Libya, controlling the trade routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-bend">Niger Bend</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Their language is unknown; Roger Blench suggests it may have been a Nilo-Saharan language, though they wrote in a Libyan script, which is only partly decipherable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest written record dates to the 5th century BCE. Herodotus calls them a very great nation who herded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and farmed dates, and says they hunted Troglodyte Ethiopians in four-horse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> to obtain slaves. Some scholars, including Sonja and Carlos Magnavita, regard such raids as possibly a speculative tale, but Roman merchants established themselves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leptis-magna">Leptis Magna</a> and Lixus to buy slaves brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>, and ostraca from the Roman fort at Bu Djem document Garamantian traders and slaves.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> wrote that the Garamantes aided the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacfarinas">Tacfarinas</a> and raided Roman coastal settlements. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-cornelius-balbus">Lucius Cornelius Balbus</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> captured fifteen of their settlements in 19 BCE, and in 202 CE Septimius Severus captured the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garama">Garama</a>. The Roman traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-maternus">Julius Maternus</a> accompanied a Garamantian king on an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agisymba">Agisymba</a>, about four months from Garama.</p><p>The kingdom spanned roughly 180,000 square kilometres. Their decline may have been connected to worsening climate or overuse of water: fossil water is non-renewable, and over centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> level fell.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The kingdom rested on a network of tunnels and shafts, known to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foggara">foggaras</a>, that tapped fossil water under the limestone layer beneath the desert sand. Their dating is disputed, between 200 BCE and 200 CE, and a system of slave labour kept them maintained. The Garamantes had about eight major towns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garama">Garama</a>, near modern Germa, had around four thousand people, with another six thousand in villages within 5 kilometres. The king controlled trade routes reaching between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-bend">Niger Bend</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>, with oases such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghat">Ghat</a> and Barkat as bases for traders. Excavated cemeteries show a population of diverse geographical origin, including some individuals of sub-Saharan origin buried among other Garamantes, some perhaps enslaved.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Garamantes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Roman traveller Julius Maternus journeyed with the king of the <b>Garamantes</b> for about four months from Garama to a land called Agisymba, whose people the king presented as his subjects.</blockquote><a href="/culture/garamantes">Garamantes · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Garamantes</b> of the Fezzan are often counted among their ancestors, and some researchers tie the rise of the Tuareg to the Garamantes’ fall; the Lamtuna, the core of the Almoravid dynasty, were close kin.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tuareg">Tuareg · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
